      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Highland sits where Highland Road, MD 216, and MD 108 meet, a crossroads still marked by farms and horse fields.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highland%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1749 White Hall estate, also called Hickory Ridge, passed through the Ridgely, Hopkins, and Disney families.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highland%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Although Highland never had a train station, it lay nine miles from Laurel’s B&O line, accessed via the rolling road now Route 216.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highland%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "William Wall opened a general store and post office in 1865, briefly naming the crossroads Walls Crossroads before it became Highland in 1878.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highland%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The downtown Highland district covers most of the 418‑acre Hickory Ridge land grant, a historic farm area.",
